// The purpose of this script
:summary: A step-by-step filming script that shows why I can afford to restore my system with a deliberately destructive process: my data never lives on my system drive, it lives on a 4-drive NAS. I image my system disk to an external USB drive with CloneZilla Live, then wipe and restore that disk from the image back to a known, tested, stable state - with a strong, immediately-delivered disclaimer that restoring OVERWRITES EVERYTHING on the drive or partition.
// An outline of this script
:description: Cold open hooks the viewer: a system I deliberately break, because I afford to. Title sequence. Scene 1 is the strong disclaimer, immediately after the title sequence: restoring overwrites EVERYTHING on the target drive or partition, no mercy, no undo, and the viewer must understand that before anything else. Scene 2 frames the setup: system and data are separated - my data lives on a 4-drive NAS in RAID-5 (4 x 6TB, 18TB usable) with a 5TB external USB drive backing the NAS up on a schedule - and I am an AI Generalist whose new installs regularly brick my system. Scene 3 covers preparing and booting the CloneZilla live rescue disk. Scene 4 walks through saving the whole system disk as an image on an external USB drive (device-image -> local_dev -> sdb1 -> savedisk). Scene 5 walks through wiping and restoring the disk from that image (restoredisk), re-emphasising the destructive nature of the step. Scene 6 recaps, re-delivers the disclaimer, delivers the core promise, the Call To Action, and the Signature Sign Off.
